WEBVTT

00:00:00.202 --> 00:00:01.436
Last video ended here.

00:00:02.123 --> 00:00:04.330
This curve, f of x equals x cubed

00:00:04.391 --> 00:00:07.159
minus three x, has a local maximum at

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.083
minus one, two.

00:00:08.966 --> 00:00:11.313
Now we only look from x equals minus

00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:14.084
two to x equals three, with both ends

00:00:14.145 --> 00:00:14.525
included.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.638
Near minus one, nothing is higher than two.

00:00:18.223 --> 00:00:20.430
But follow the curve to the right-hand end.

00:00:21.134 --> 00:00:23.490
At x equals three, f is eighteen.

00:00:24.055 --> 00:00:26.803
So a local maximum isn't always the largest

00:00:26.864 --> 00:00:27.204
value.

00:00:27.947 --> 00:00:29.912
How do we find the largest and smallest

00:00:29.973 --> 00:00:32.077
values on a whole interval, and make sure

00:00:32.118 --> 00:00:32.899
we haven't missed one?

00:00:33.529 --> 00:00:36.059
The largest value over the whole domain is

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.686
called the global maximum.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:38.769
A point

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:41.851
a, f of a, is a global maximum

00:00:42.172 --> 00:00:43.852
if f of x is less than or

00:00:43.993 --> 00:00:46.273
equal to f of a, for every x

00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:47.014
in the domain.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.052
Picture a horizontal line at that height.

00:00:50.656 --> 00:00:52.623
For a global maximum, no part of the

00:00:52.664 --> 00:00:53.607
graph pokes above it.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.317
Put the line at two, the local maximum,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.827
and the curve pokes through near the right-hand

00:00:58.928 --> 00:00:59.068
end.

00:00:59.711 --> 00:01:01.797
Put it at eighteen, and the whole graph

00:01:01.938 --> 00:01:03.201
sits on or below it.

00:01:03.945 --> 00:01:06.012
A global minimum is the same idea with

00:01:06.053 --> 00:01:06.534
a floor.

00:01:07.037 --> 00:01:08.644
No part of the graph dips below it.

00:01:09.289 --> 00:01:10.836
So where can the highest point be?

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.065
Suppose it's inside the interval, where the curve

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:14.507
is smooth.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.104
Then its tangent must be flat.

00:01:17.991 --> 00:01:20.216
If the tangent sloped either way, you could

00:01:20.337 --> 00:01:22.200
step a little uphill and find a higher

00:01:22.261 --> 00:01:22.501
point.

00:01:23.185 --> 00:01:25.561
So inside, look at stationary points.

00:01:26.326 --> 00:01:28.414
But at an end, the curve just stops.

00:01:29.098 --> 00:01:30.905
It doesn't need to be flat, because the

00:01:31.107 --> 00:01:32.875
uphill direction leaves the domain.

00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:35.884
And at a sharp corner, there's no single

00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:39.553
tangent, so f prime doesn't exist there, and

00:01:39.614 --> 00:01:41.939
solving f prime equals zero can't find it.

00:01:42.822 --> 00:01:44.364
On a closed interval where the curve is

00:01:44.505 --> 00:01:48.190
unbroken, the highest and lowest values always exist,

00:01:48.651 --> 00:01:50.012
and each one is at one of these

00:01:50.073 --> 00:01:50.533
candidates.

00:01:51.276 --> 00:01:53.603
If the curve breaks somewhere, examine that point

00:01:53.645 --> 00:01:53.844
too.

00:01:54.543 --> 00:01:55.918
That gives a short method.

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:57.264
List the candidates:

00:01:57.825 --> 00:02:01.254
stationary points inside the interval, any interior points

00:02:01.314 --> 00:02:02.778
where f prime doesn't exist,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.383
and both endpoints.

00:02:05.005 --> 00:02:07.321
Then work out f at every one, and

00:02:07.362 --> 00:02:07.703
compare.

00:02:08.648 --> 00:02:11.438
Here, f prime of x is three, times

00:02:11.619 --> 00:02:13.907
x minus one, times x plus one.

00:02:14.389 --> 00:02:16.961
So the stationary points are minus one and

00:02:17.163 --> 00:02:18.308
one, both inside.

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:21.040
The endpoints are minus two and three,

00:02:21.563 --> 00:02:23.310
and f prime exists everywhere.

00:02:24.213 --> 00:02:27.300
The values are minus two, two, minus two

00:02:27.321 --> 00:02:28.442
and eighteen.

00:02:29.085 --> 00:02:31.834
The largest is eighteen, so the global maximum

00:02:31.956 --> 00:02:34.103
is eighteen, at x equals three.

00:02:34.846 --> 00:02:37.153
The smallest is minus two, and it happens

00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:37.594
twice,

00:02:38.117 --> 00:02:39.380
at x equals minus two

00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:40.945
and x equals one.

00:02:41.829 --> 00:02:43.816
Notice we never had to classify anything.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.596
Comparing the values settles it.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.243
Now a worked example.

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:50.236
Find the global maximum and minimum of f

00:02:50.257 --> 00:02:52.663
of x equals x plus four over x,

00:02:53.025 --> 00:02:54.609
for x from a half to four.

00:02:55.353 --> 00:02:55.632
First,

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:58.520
f prime of x is one minus four

00:02:58.682 --> 00:02:59.463
over x squared.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:02.374
Setting that to zero gives x squared equals

00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:05.386
four, so x is two or minus two.

00:03:06.091 --> 00:03:08.024
Minus two isn't in the interval, so reject

00:03:08.045 --> 00:03:08.144
it.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.531
And f isn't defined at zero, but zero

00:03:11.652 --> 00:03:12.772
isn't in the interval either,

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.293
so the curve is unbroken here, with no

00:03:15.354 --> 00:03:15.714
corners.

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:19.004
The candidates are a half, two and four.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:21.716
Now evaluate f at each one.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.844
f of a half is a half plus

00:03:24.926 --> 00:03:26.509
eight, which is eight and a half.

00:03:27.412 --> 00:03:29.519
f of two is two plus two,

00:03:30.021 --> 00:03:30.241
four.

00:03:31.106 --> 00:03:33.218
f of four is four plus one,

00:03:33.742 --> 00:03:34.063
five.

00:03:34.936 --> 00:03:35.385
Compare them.

00:03:35.988 --> 00:03:38.594
The global minimum is four, at the stationary

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:40.138
point x equals two.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.164
The global maximum is eight and a half,

00:03:43.505 --> 00:03:46.069
at the endpoint x equals a half, where

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.512
the tangent isn't flat at all.

00:03:48.174 --> 00:03:50.922
Solving f prime equals zero alone would have

00:03:51.003 --> 00:03:51.804
missed it completely.

00:03:52.637 --> 00:03:54.252
The same method works for an exponential.

00:03:54.896 --> 00:03:56.921
Find the greatest and least values of g

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.770
of x equals x e to the minus

00:03:59.971 --> 00:04:02.116
x, for x from zero to three.

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:04.502
By the product rule, g prime of x

00:04:04.704 --> 00:04:06.827
is e to the minus x, times one

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:07.429
minus x.

00:04:08.172 --> 00:04:09.899
e to the minus x is never zero,

00:04:10.101 --> 00:04:12.451
so the only stationary point is x equals

00:04:12.673 --> 00:04:12.772
one.

00:04:13.417 --> 00:04:15.813
The candidates are zero, one and three.

00:04:16.561 --> 00:04:17.671
g of zero is zero.

00:04:18.116 --> 00:04:20.302
g of one is one over e, about

00:04:20.543 --> 00:04:21.485
point three six eight.

00:04:22.068 --> 00:04:24.799
g of three is three over e cubed,

00:04:25.162 --> 00:04:26.628
about point one four nine.

00:04:27.331 --> 00:04:29.156
So the greatest value is one over e,

00:04:29.398 --> 00:04:31.444
at x equals one, and the least is

00:04:31.485 --> 00:04:33.992
zero, at the endpoint x equals zero.

00:04:34.775 --> 00:04:37.604
Flats, ends and corners, then compare.

00:04:38.349 --> 00:04:41.085
Flats are the stationary points inside the interval.

00:04:41.932 --> 00:04:43.487
Ends are the two endpoints.

00:04:44.193 --> 00:04:47.039
Include every interior point where f prime doesn't

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:48.423
exist, such as a corner.

00:04:49.387 --> 00:04:52.355
Then compare the values of f itself, not

00:04:52.597 --> 00:04:53.017
f prime.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.666
The list is complete on a closed interval

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:57.212
where the curve is unbroken.

00:04:59.861 --> 00:05:00.350
Your turn.

00:05:00.755 --> 00:05:03.476
Find the global maximum and minimum values of

00:05:03.757 --> 00:05:06.757
f of x equals x cubed minus twelve

00:05:06.878 --> 00:05:09.179
x, for x from zero to three,

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:10.680
and say where they occur.

00:05:11.523 --> 00:05:12.409
Pause here to answer.

00:05:23.181 --> 00:05:25.465
f prime of x is three, times x

00:05:25.505 --> 00:05:27.809
minus two, times x plus two,

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.674
which is zero at two and minus two.

00:05:31.378 --> 00:05:33.431
Minus two isn't in the interval, so reject

00:05:33.492 --> 00:05:33.572
it.

00:05:34.417 --> 00:05:37.380
The candidates are zero, two and three, giving

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.862
zero, minus sixteen and minus nine.

00:05:40.603 --> 00:05:43.007
So the global maximum is zero, at x

00:05:43.048 --> 00:05:45.553
equals zero, and the global minimum is minus

00:05:45.594 --> 00:05:47.316
sixteen, at x equals two.

00:05:48.041 --> 00:05:49.998
Sixteen, from minus two, doesn't count.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:51.175
It's outside the domain.

00:05:52.763 --> 00:05:53.148
Last one.

00:05:53.892 --> 00:05:55.695
For h of x equals e to the

00:05:55.875 --> 00:05:58.719
x, minus two x, for x from zero

00:05:58.779 --> 00:05:59.360
to two,

00:05:59.941 --> 00:06:02.966
find the exact global maximum and minimum values.

00:06:03.749 --> 00:06:04.677
Pause here to answer.

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:17.130
h prime of x is e to the

00:06:17.271 --> 00:06:18.092
x minus two,

00:06:18.734 --> 00:06:20.657
zero when x is the natural log of

00:06:20.718 --> 00:06:22.039
two, inside the interval.

00:06:23.144 --> 00:06:24.072
h of zero is one.

00:06:25.135 --> 00:06:26.718
h of the natural log of two is

00:06:26.879 --> 00:06:28.883
two, minus two times the natural log of

00:06:28.944 --> 00:06:30.587
two, about point six one.

00:06:31.712 --> 00:06:34.101
h of two is e squared minus four,

00:06:34.544 --> 00:06:35.728
about three point three nine.

00:06:36.431 --> 00:06:38.601
So the maximum is at x equals two,

00:06:39.044 --> 00:06:40.631
and the minimum is at the natural log

00:06:40.652 --> 00:06:40.952
of two.

00:06:41.676 --> 00:06:43.764
Zero is h prime there, not h.

00:06:45.508 --> 00:06:48.435
So on a closed interval, list every candidate

00:06:48.676 --> 00:06:50.039
and compare the values of f.

00:06:50.661 --> 00:06:52.982
That completes this run of differential calculus,

00:06:53.523 --> 00:06:55.244
from the derivative of e to the x,

00:06:55.705 --> 00:06:57.826
all the way to global maxima and minima.

00:06:58.609 --> 00:07:00.741
Later in the course, the function comes from

00:07:00.782 --> 00:07:01.807
a real situation.

00:07:02.512 --> 00:07:04.963
Forty metres of fencing make three sides of

00:07:05.004 --> 00:07:06.550
a rectangle, against a wall.

00:07:07.215 --> 00:07:09.123
What shape gives the largest area?

00:07:12.035 --> 00:07:12.837
Here are the key notes.

00:07:13.481 --> 00:07:15.208
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:15.751
to copy them.
